Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; gothic supernatural imagery.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A brass sign reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. Beyond the open office door, BOB CRATCHIT, a slight, pale clerk in a threadbare coat and white comforter, copies letters beside a fire no larger than one coal.

EBENEZER SCROOGE, a thin elderly man with a sharp nose, wiry gray hair, blue lips, and a black coat, guards the coal-box from his desk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Scrooge was his sole executor, his sole friend, and sole mourner.

FRED, Scrooge's rosy, handsome, energetic nephew, bursts in from the fog.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle? You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with “Merry Christmas” on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding!

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, I say, God bless it!

Scrooge turns back to his ledger.

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

Fred leaves without an angry word. Bob returns Fred's greeting warmly. Scrooge hears it and scowls.

MR. LAMBERT, a portly charity gentleman with a ledger, enters with another gentleman.

MR. LAMBERT
At this festive season, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. LAMBERT
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. LAMBERT
They are.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR. LAMBERT
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
It's enough for a man to understand his own business, and not to interfere with other people's. Good afternoon.

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his accounts as the last spark dies in Bob's grate.

EXT. SCROOGE'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Fog and frost choke a narrow London yard. Scrooge trudges through it alone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To edge his way along the crowded paths of life, warning all human sympathy to keep its distance, was nuts to Scrooge.

He fits his key into the enormous door knocker.

The knocker becomes the livid, spectacled face of JACOB MARLEY.

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He turns the key, enters, and slams the door. The sound thunders through the empty house.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge double-locks the door, checks beneath the table, sofa, bed, and inside the closet. All is empty. He sits beside a tiny fire with a bowl of gruel.

A disused bell begins to swing.

Every bell in the house rings at once. Then comes a deep clanking from below: a heavy chain dragged over cellar casks, up the stairs, and toward the door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The door opens without a hand.

JACOB MARLEY enters: transparent, livid, wearing his old pigtail, waistcoat, tights, and boots. A chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses winds around his body.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

JACOB MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

JACOB MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

JACOB MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects the senses. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley shakes his chain. The fire gutters.

JACOB MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it.

SCROOGE
Why do spirits walk the earth?

JACOB MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

Marley lifts the chain.

JACOB MARLEY
Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were, all, my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

SCROOGE
Jacob, tell me more. Speak comfort to me!

JACOB MARLEY
I have none to give. I am here to warn you that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me.

JACOB MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Expect the first tomorrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

JACOB MARLEY
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

The window flies open. Marley backs into the night.

Outside, chained phantoms drift through the fog, moaning. One reaches toward a woman and infant below, but cannot touch them.

Scrooge slams the window shut. He tries to say “Humbug,” but stops at the first syllable.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge lies awake in his dressing gown and nightcap. The church clock strikes the quarters, then ONE.

A clear white light floods the room. The bed curtains draw aside.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands beside him: child-sized yet ancient, with white hair, a youthful face, bare feet, a white tunic trimmed with summer flowers, a holly branch, and a jet of light rising from its head beneath an extinguisher cap.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

The Spirit extends a strong hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation, then. Take heed!

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this.

The Spirit touches Scrooge's heart.

The bedroom wall dissolves into a snow-covered country road.

INT. OLD SCHOOLROOM - DAY

An abandoned schoolroom materializes around Scrooge. A lonely YOUNG SCROOGE sits reading beside a feeble fire.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

The Spirit points. The schoolroom door bursts open and FAN, Scrooge's small, bright-eyed sister, rushes in.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! To bring you home, home, home!

YOUNG SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Yes! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. Home's like Heaven! We're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

Fan embraces Young Scrooge and pulls him toward the door.

The vision shifts. A boy outside the school sings a Christmas carol through the fog.

Scrooge reaches into his pocket, ashamed.

SCROOGE
Poor boy! I wish—but it's too late now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

The bare warehouse bursts into warmth and light. FEZZIWIG, a jovial old master in a Welsh wig and capacious waistcoat, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Young Scrooge and DICK WILKINS throw open the shutters. The warehouse becomes a crowded Christmas ballroom. FEZZIWIG and MRS. FEZZIWIG lead a dancing set while fiddles, laughter, cake, meat, and beer fill the room.

Scrooge watches, laughing and weeping.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter. He has spent but a few pounds. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome, a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The dance whirls faster. Fezziwig bows to every departing guest.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOR - NIGHT

A young woman, BELLE, sits opposite a younger Scrooge. The Ghost's light trembles across her tears.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

Scrooge's younger self lowers his eyes.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle turns away.

The parlor fills with a happy family: Belle as a mother, children running, and her husband, HENRY BELLE, returning with Christmas parcels.

HENRY BELLE
I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE
Who was it?

HENRY BELLE
Mr. Scrooge. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

The family laughter becomes distant.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home!

He presses the extinguisher cap over the Ghost's bright head. Light streams beneath it, then vanishes.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge awakes in the middle of a snore. The bell strikes ONE.

The room floods with ruddy light. Its walls become a living grove of holly, ivy, and mistletoe. A roaring fire illuminates a feast piled with turkeys, geese, joints of meat, sausages, pies, puddings, fruit, and punch.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits upon the feast like a jolly giant. He wears a green robe bordered with white fur, a holly wreath, and an empty, rusted sword sheath.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! And know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I have never seen the like of you before.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. I have more than eighteen hundred brothers.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

The Ghost rises.

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge grips the robe.

The feast, fire, room, and night vanish.

INT. CRATCHIT FAMILY ROOM - DAY

The Cratchit family room is small but bright with Christmas. MRS. CRATCHIT, in a twice-turned gown with brave ribbons, lays the cloth. PETER CRATCHIT attacks a saucepan of potatoes. MARTHA and the younger children bustle around a table.

BOB CRATCHIT enters in a threadbare coat and long comforter, carrying TINY TIM, a frail boy with a little crutch and an iron support frame.

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family sets down a goose, potatoes, gravy, and a blazing pudding. They sit.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

The family echoes him.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge watches Tiny Tim's small hand in Bob's.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows his head. The Spirit's words have become his own.

BOB CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

The family forces a toast, then recovers its laughter. Scrooge keeps his eyes on Tiny Tim until the light fades.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

FRED'S dining room glows with firelight, music, and a crowded Christmas party. CLARA, Fred's cheerful wife, raises a glass.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

CLARA
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, that's the truth. However, his offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried.

The family laughs. A harp plays a simple air from Scrooge's childhood.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it.

Scrooge laughs with them, though they cannot hear him.

FRED
Uncle Scrooge! A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is!

Scrooge lifts an invisible glass.

EXT. LONDON STREET - NIGHT

Snow falls over the city. The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown visibly older. He leads Scrooge past warm windows, crowded homes, a miner's hut, a lighthouse, and a ship at sea.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night.

SCROOGE
To-night!

The Ghost opens his robe.

Two children cling to it: a ragged, yellow, wolfish boy and girl.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The clock strikes TWELVE.

The Ghost of Christmas Present vanishes. A tall black shape approaches through the snow.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME stands before Scrooge, shrouded entirely in black save for one outstretched hand.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come?

The Phantom points onward.

SCROOGE
Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me, I know.

The hand points to the door.

EXT. ROYAL EXCHANGE - DAY

The Phantom and Scrooge stand among merchants counting money and consulting watches.

MERCHANT HARRIS, a fat man with a monstrous chin, speaks with MERCHANT JONES and MERCHANT BROWN.

MERCHANT HARRIS
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT JONES
When did he die?

MERCHANT BROWN
What has he done with his money?

MERCHANT HARRIS
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

The merchants laugh.

MERCHANT HARRIS
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

They stroll away. Scrooge searches the crowd for himself. The Phantom points toward a filthy alley.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

OLD JOE, a gray-haired rascal beside a charcoal stove, opens a bundle for MRS. HIGGINS, a bold charwoman; MRS. DILBER, a laundress; and MR. SLOANE, an undertaker's man.

MRS. HIGGINS
What odds then, Mrs. Dilber? Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

MRS. DILBER
That's true, indeed.

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

MR. SLOANE lays out seals, a pencil-case, sleeve-buttons, and a brooch. Mrs. Dilber produces sheets, towels, silver teaspoons, sugar-tongs, and boots.

Mrs. Higgins unrolls dark bed-curtains.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. HIGGINS
Yes, I do. Why not? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say.

OLD JOE
His blankets?

MRS. HIGGINS
Whose else's do you think?

They divide coins on the floor and laugh over the spoils.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

The shop dissolves into a bare, dark room.

A sheet covers a body on a bed. No one watches it. A cat scratches at the door. Rats gnaw beneath the hearth.

The Phantom points to the covered head.

SCROOGE
This is a fearful place. Let us go!

The hand does not move.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The black robe spreads like a wing.

INT. CRATCHIT FAMILY ROOM - DAY

The same room, years ahead. A vacant chair and a carefully kept crutch stand beside the hearth.

The noisy Cratchit children are silent. PETER reads from a book. MRS. CRATCHIT and MARTHA sew.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

PETER CRATCHIT
I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

Bob enters alone. He looks at the empty chair.

BOB CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. It was no trouble. No trouble.

He touches the crutch.

BOB CRATCHIT
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

The family gathers around him.

BOB CRATCHIT
When we recollect how patient and how mild he was, although he was a little, little child, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it.

He breaks down, then steadies himself.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy.

Scrooge turns desperately to the Phantom.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead!

The Phantom points onward.

EXT. NEGLECTED CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A weed-choked churchyard lies between dark houses. The Phantom points to a neglected grave.

Scrooge approaches. The stone bears the name EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Phantom's hand remains fixed.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge, and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

He falls to his knees and clutches the black robe.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows by an altered life!

The Phantom's hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

Scrooge clings to the spectral hand.

The Phantom shrinks, collapses, and dwindles into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed is his own. The sunlight is clear and bright.

Scrooge scrambles out of bed, his face wet with tears.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He embraces a bed curtain.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here. I am here. The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled!

Church bells explode across the morning.

Scrooge throws open the window. TOM, a boy in Sunday clothes, looks up from the street.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM
Why, Christmas Day.

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one? The big Turkey hanging there?

TOM
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it. Tell 'em to bring it here. I'll give you a shilling, and come back with the man in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Tom races away.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it.

Scrooge laughs until he cries.

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge walks in his best clothes, smiling at every passerby.

MR. LAMBERT approaches with his ledger. Scrooge takes both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

Scrooge whispers an astonishing figure in his ear.

MR. LAMBERT
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

MR. LAMBERT
I will!

Scrooge turns toward Fred's house, walking faster.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

Fred and Clara prepare a lavish Christmas table.

SCROOGE
Fred!

Fred turns, startled.

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred rushes to embrace him.

A moment later, the room is full of music, dancing, and laughter. Scrooge plays every game with the joy of a schoolboy.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The next morning. Scrooge sits behind his desk with the door wide open.

BOB CRATCHIT hurries in, eighteen minutes late, and drops onto his stool.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob edges nearer to the ruler.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares at him, terrified.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob's fear breaks into stunned laughter. Scrooge claps him on the back.

INT. CRATCHIT FAMILY ROOM - DAY

Later, Tiny Tim, alive and smiling, sits beside Bob with his crutch. Scrooge serves the family from a generous Christmas table, awkwardly delighted.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. And to Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

Bob raises his glass. Tiny Tim looks toward Scrooge.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

FADE OUT.

THE END
